From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 14 Jun 2026 14:55:20 -0700
Subject: [RFC EXTENSIONS 0/3] net: flow_dissector: VLAN / QinQ / VXLAN-inner extensions to the opt-in fast-path
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

These three patches are draft extensions to the
"opt-in fast-path for eth + IPv{4,6} + {TCP,UDP}" series (now in
net-next as commit 8013aee91ccb). They cover the highest-leverage
shapes the bare eth+IP fast-path doesn't match today.

Status: drafts. They apply on top of the v2-inline base, build, and
follow the same code structure as the parent series, but have not
been put through selftests or run on the cake-soak rig. Posted for
discussion of design and scope; expect them to need polishing
before landing.

Cross-reference doc:
kernel-patches/series3-flowdis-fastpath/docs/packet-flow-context.md
sections 9 and 10 (in the same repository) cover the eligibility
checklist, recipe, and value-vs-effort ranking these patches act
on.

Patch 1 -- single VLAN
  Adds a one-tag fast-path covering Eth + (ETH_P_8021Q |
  ETH_P_8021AD) + IPv4/IPv6 + TCP/UDP. Handles both the
  hardware-stripped form (skb_vlan_tag_present + skb->protocol) and
  the in-band form (4-byte vlan_hdr at nhoff). Writes
  FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_VLAN and optionally
  FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_NUM_OF_VLANS. After the tag, control falls
  through to the existing IPv4 / IPv6 fast-path bodies.

  Byte-identical with the slow path for the shapes handled. Adds
  ~3-5 ns on a single-VLAN packet to the IPv4 fast-path body,
  saves ~30 ns total versus the slow path (graph walk +
  proto_again rewind avoided).

  Highest-leverage extension: VLAN-tagged traffic is dominant in
  most data-centre LAN deployments and the bare eth+IP fast-path
  never matched it.

Patch 2 -- QinQ (incremental over patch 1)
  Lifts patch 1's depth limit from 1 to 2 so two stacked tags (the
  canonical S-TAG + C-TAG QinQ shape) are also fast-pathed. The
  outer tag is written to FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_VLAN, the inner to
  FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_CVLAN, mirroring the slow path's state
  machine (dissector_vlan MAX -> VLAN -> CVLAN).

  Byte-identical. Targets service-provider and carrier-grade
  deployments where QinQ is the wire format.

Patch 3 -- VXLAN inner (RFC EXPERIMENT, behaviour change)
  THIS PATCH IS A BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, not a byte-identical fast-path.
  The standard flow_keys_dissector stops at outer UDP today, so for
  VXLAN traffic between two VTEPs every inner flow collapses onto a
  single 5-tuple bucket -- bad for cake/fq fairness, RPS, RFS, ECMP
  in k8s overlay deployments.

  This patch makes the fast-path descend into the VXLAN inner Eth +
  IPv4/IPv6 + TCP/UDP when outer UDP dst port == IANA_VXLAN_UDP_PORT
  (4789). FLOW_DIS_ENCAPSULATION is set; inner addrs/ports/basic
  overwrite outer; same shape as __skb_flow_dissect_gre()'s
  slow-path descent.

  Posted alongside patches 1 and 2 because k8s overlay hashing is a
  real operational problem worth a public discussion. Open
  questions in the commit message:

    - Should this be a separate sysctl
      (net.core.flow_dissector_descend_udp_encap) rather than
      piggy-backed on the fast-path gate?
    - Should the slow path also get UDP-encap descent so the
      contract stays "fast-path output == slow-path output"?
    - How should the configured VXLAN port(s) be discovered
      (today hardcoded to 4789; production needs per-netns
      lookup)?

  Not landable as-is. Posted as a foothold for the discussion.

Bases on net-next (HEAD = parent series + these three commits):

  8013aee91ccb  net: flow_dissector: add opt-in fast-path for
                eth + IPv{4,6} + {TCP,UDP}   (parent, in net-next)
   ^
  76e3e4647731  this patch 1 (single VLAN)
  c984b9f514b9  this patch 2 (QinQ)
  b616176231dc  this patch 3 (VXLAN inner, RFC EXPERIMENT)

Each patch is +60 / +17 / +46 lines respectively, all in
net/core/flow_dissector.c only. No header changes, no new sysctls,
no new exports.

Follow-ups not in this set:
  - PPPoE (ISP edges; structurally similar to single-VLAN).
  - MPLS single-label (SP networks; harder because depth varies).
  - GTP-U (telco; harder because extension headers vary).
  - Selftests at tools/testing/selftests/net/ comparing fast-path
    output to slow-path output byte-by-byte for VLAN / QinQ.

